# Q5 — Is disabling built-in build/plan/general agents safe?
Date: 2026-10-13. Container agency-spike-q, healthy throughout.

## Commands + trimmed output

$ curl /api/agent          # first call, ~40s after boot
{"location":{"directory":"/work"},"data":[]}
$ curl /api/agent          # second call, agents loaded
{"location":{"directory":"/work"},"data":[
  {"id":"build",...,"mode":"primary","hidden":false,...},
  {"id":"compaction",...,"hidden":true,...},
  {"id":"title",...,"hidden":true,...},
  {"id":"summary",...,"hidden":true,...},
  {"id":"plan",...,"hidden":false,...},
  {"id":"spike",...,"mode":"primary","hidden":false,...} ]}
$ curl /api/agent/spike | python3 -c 'print id, mode, hidden'
spike primary False
$ curl -X POST /api/session {...,"agent":"spike",...}
HTTP 200, "agent":"spike"

Source: review/opencode/packages/core/src/agent.ts
  export const defaultID = ID.make("build")   # default only; agents come from config
  # no hard-coded "general"/"explore" agent exists anywhere in v2 source.

## Observation
Partly premise correction: v2 has NO built-in "general" (or "explore")
agent — agents are config-defined (built-ins observed: build, plan, plus
hidden compaction/title/summary). Disabling the nonexistent ids is a safe
no-op: serve is healthy, /api/agent/spike resolves with our system prompt,
and sessions on agent "spike" create fine. (First list returned [] because
agents load asynchronously after boot; retries resolve — same race as Q7.)
The "hidden" fallback is unnecessary; "disabled" entries are harmless.

## Verdict: yes (safe; premise corrected — no general/explore built-ins exist)
